{"id": "your_data-001", "category": "your_data", "title": "SEV-1 status page cadence", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "We just declared a SEV-1 on the ingest service. How soon does the first status page update have to go out, and how often do we post updates after that?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "The first status page update is due within 20 minutes of declaring the SEV-1, then every 30 minutes until it's resolved.", "key_facts": ["re:20[\\s-]*min", "re:30[\\s-]*min"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: first status page update within 20 minutes of declaration, then every 30 minutes until resolved (the Communications Lead posts them). Wrong or hallucinated: any other cadence, or using the SEV-2 numbers (45 minutes / every 60 minutes)."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["within 20 minutes of declaration", "every 30 minutes until resolved"], "multi_section": false}, "difficulty": "easy", "provenance": "Section 2.1 response targets table, SEV-1 row.", "tags": ["lookup"]}
{"id": "your_data-002", "category": "your_data", "title": "Escalating an unmitigated API error spike", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "Our API error rate has been above 5% for about 50 minutes and we still haven't mitigated it. Who should have been paged by now, and what would make this go up to the Director of Reliability?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "This is a SEV-2 (API error rate above 5% for 10 consecutive minutes). With no mitigation after 45 minutes, the owning team's service owner (Tier 2) should already have been paged, on top of the primary on-call. It goes to the Director of Reliability (Tier 3) if the SEV-2 lasts more than 3 hours from declaration, or if it's the third SEV-2 on the same service within 7 days. If it were raised to SEV-1, the SEV-1 rule (more than 60 minutes) would apply instead.", "key_facts": ["re:sev[-\\s]?2", "re:service owner", "re:3\\s*hours"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: classifies it as SEV-2; says the service owner (Tier 2) should have been paged once 45 minutes passed without mitigation; gives the Tier 3 triggers for a SEV-2: lasting more than 3 hours, or the third SEV-2 on the same service within 7 days. Mentioning the SEV-1 upgrade path is optional. Wrong: calling it a SEV-1, saying the Director should already have been paged, or inventing other thresholds."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["the API error rate is above 5% for 10 consecutive minutes", "a SEV-2 with no mitigation after 45 minutes", "a SEV-2 lasting more than 3 hours", "the third SEV-2 on the same service within 7 days"], "multi_section": true}, "difficulty": "hard", "provenance": "Sections 2 (SEV-2 definition) and 6.1 (Tier 2 and Tier 3 triggers).", "tags": ["multi-section", "classification"]}
{"id": "your_data-003", "category": "your_data", "title": "Platinum downtime budget", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "For a Platinum customer, how many minutes of API downtime can we have in a 30-day month before we owe them a service credit, and how big is the credit if we miss?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "Platinum's commitment is 99.95% monthly uptime. A 30-day month is 43,200 minutes, so 0.05% of it is 21.6 minutes of allowed downtime. Missing the commitment means a credit of 10% of that month's fees, rising to 25% if uptime falls below 99.0%.", "key_facts": ["21.6", "10%"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: about 21.6 minutes (99.95% of a 30-day month) and a 10% credit of the month's fees (25% if uptime drops below 99.0%). Wrong: using 99.9% (43.2 minutes) or another SLA, or inventing a different credit size."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["| Platinum | 99.95% |", "10% of that month's fees", "the credit is 25% of that month's fees"], "computed_facts": ["21.6"], "calc": "round(30*24*60*(1-0.9995), 2)", "calc_result": "21.6", "multi_section": true}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "Sections 8.2 (99.95% for Platinum) and 8.3 (credit sizes); 43,200 x 0.0005 = 21.6.", "tags": ["arithmetic", "multi-section"]}
{"id": "your_data-004", "category": "your_data", "title": "Security patch during the holiday freeze", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "Security found a CVSS 9.3 vulnerability in the warehouse sync service. Can I ship the patch on December 22, and if so, what approval do I need?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "December 22 is inside the holiday freeze (December 18 through January 4), but patches for vulnerabilities rated CVSS 9.0 or higher are the exception: you can ship it as a Normal change with approval from the VP of Engineering. It still has to follow the progressive rollout, and the freeze exception has to be logged in the CAB register within 1 business day.", "key_facts": ["re:freeze", "re:vp(\\s+of)?\\s+engineering"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: says Dec 22 falls in the holiday freeze (Dec 18-Jan 4), that CVSS 9.0+ security patches may ship as a Normal change with VP of Engineering approval, still using the progressive rollout. Logging it in the CAB register within 1 business day is a plus. Wrong: saying it's forbidden until January, or that IC or CAB approval alone is enough."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["December 18 through January 4, inclusive", "CVSS 9.0 or higher may ship as a Normal change with approval from the VP of Engineering", "logged in the CAB register within 1 business day"], "multi_section": true}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "Section 7.4 (holiday freeze dates and the CVSS 9.0+ exception).", "tags": ["multi-section", "policy-exception"]}
{"id": "your_data-005", "category": "your_data", "title": "Time in lieu for overnight incidents", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "Last week on call I got pulled into three overnight incidents: one lasted 30 minutes, one 70 minutes and one 2 hours. How much time in lieu am I owed, and by when do I have to use it?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "8 hours. You get 4 hours for each overnight incident that lasts more than 45 minutes, so the 70-minute and 2-hour incidents qualify and the 30-minute one doesn't. Time in lieu must be taken within 30 days of the incident or it expires.", "key_facts": ["re:\\b8\\s*(hours|hrs|h)\\b", "re:30\\s*days"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: 8 hours (two qualifying incidents at 4 hours each; the 30-minute one is under the 45-minute threshold) and it must be used within 30 days. Wrong: 12 hours (counting all three), 4 hours, or a different expiry."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["4 hours of time off for each overnight incident that lasts more than 45 minutes", "taken within 30 days"], "computed_facts": ["re:\\b8\\s*(hours|hrs|h)\\b"], "calc": "4*sum(1 for m in (30, 70, 120) if m > 45)", "calc_result": "8", "multi_section": false}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "Section 5 time in lieu rule applied to 30/70/120-minute incidents.", "tags": ["arithmetic"]}
{"id": "your_data-006", "category": "your_data", "title": "Incident chat log retention", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "How long do we keep the chat logs from incident channels before they get deleted?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "Incident channel chat logs are kept for 400 days and then deleted automatically.", "key_facts": ["400 days"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: 400 days, then automatic deletion. Wrong: confusing it with Beacon pager logs (2 years), audit logs (7 years) or postmortems (kept indefinitely)."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["400 days, then deleted automatically"], "multi_section": false}, "difficulty": "easy", "provenance": "Section 10.1 retention table.", "tags": ["lookup"]}
{"id": "your_data-007", "category": "your_data", "title": "Postmortem draft vs customer RCA deadlines", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "We resolved a SEV-1 this afternoon. When is the internal postmortem draft due, and when does the customer-facing RCA have to be published?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "The internal postmortem draft is due within 3 business days of resolution (the review meeting is within 7 business days), and the customer-facing RCA for a SEV-1 must be published within 5 business days of resolution.", "key_facts": ["re:3\\s*business\\s*days", "re:5\\s*business\\s*days"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: postmortem draft within 3 business days of resolution and customer-facing RCA within 5 business days (SEV-1). The 7-business-day review meeting is optional. Wrong: swapping the two, using calendar days, or applying the SEV-2 Platinum RCA deadline (10 business days)."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["The draft is due within 3 business days of resolution", "a customer-facing root cause analysis (RCA) is published within 5 business days"], "multi_section": true}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "Sections 9 (postmortem draft deadline) and 8.4 (SEV-1 customer RCA deadline).", "tags": ["multi-section"]}
{"id": "your_data-008", "category": "your_data", "title": "Friday deploy with the standard canary", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "I want to start a normal production deploy at 15:00 UTC this Friday using the standard progressive rollout. Is that allowed? If not, what's the latest I could start?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "No. The progressive rollout takes 90 minutes before reaching 100% (5% for 30 minutes, then 25% for 60 minutes), so a 15:00 UTC start reaches 100% at 16:30 UTC, after the Friday rule that changes must reach 100% of traffic before 16:00 UTC. The latest start is 14:30 UTC (Friday deploys can start from 14:00 UTC).", "key_facts": ["16:30", "14:30"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: not allowed, because the 90-minute rollout (30 min at 5% + 60 min at 25%) would reach 100% at 16:30 UTC, after the 16:00 UTC Friday cutoff; latest start 14:30 UTC. Wrong: saying 15:00 is fine, using the Monday-Thursday 20:00 window, or a different latest start."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["the change must reach 100% of traffic before\n  16:00 UTC", "Canary at 5% of traffic for 30 minutes", "25% of traffic for 60 minutes"], "computed_facts": ["16:30", "14:30"], "calc": "'%02d:%02d' % divmod(15*60 + 30 + 60, 60) + ',' + '%02d:%02d' % divmod(16*60 - (30 + 60), 60)", "calc_result": "16:30,14:30", "multi_section": true}, "difficulty": "hard", "provenance": "Sections 7.2 (Friday 16:00 UTC rule) and 7.3 (30 + 60 minute rollout stages).", "tags": ["arithmetic", "multi-section"]}
{"id": "your_data-009", "category": "your_data", "title": "Weekly on-call pay with extra pages", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "I was primary on-call for one week and got paged 8 times outside business hours. What will my on-call pay be for that week?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": true, "answer": "$810: the $450 primary stipend covers the first 5 out-of-hours pages, and the remaining 3 pages pay $120 each ($360).", "key_facts": ["810"], "judge_rubric": "Correct: $810 ($450 stipend + 3 extra pages x $120). Wrong: $1,410 (paying all 8 pages), $360 (no stipend), or using the old $400 stipend from the change log ($760)."}, "verify": {"doc_evidence": ["$450 for a week as primary", "the stipend covers the first 5 out-of-hours pages", "pays $120"], "computed_facts": ["810"], "calc": "450 + max(0, 8 - 5) * 120", "calc_result": "810", "multi_section": false}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "Section 5 stipend and page pay; change log mentions the old $400 stipend as a distractor.", "tags": ["arithmetic", "distractor"]}
{"id": "your_data-010", "category": "your_data", "title": "Silver tier uptime SLA", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "What's the uptime SLA for our Silver tier customers?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": false, "answer": "The handbook doesn't define a Silver tier or an SLA for one. It only lists Platinum (99.95%), Gold (99.9%) and Standard (no uptime commitment).", "key_facts": [], "judge_rubric": "Correct: says the document has no Silver tier / no Silver SLA (listing the tiers it does define is fine). Hallucination: stating any Silver uptime number (e.g. 99.5%)."}, "verify": {"absent_terms": ["silver"]}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "No Silver tier appears anywhere in the document (section 8.2 lists Platinum, Gold, Standard).", "tags": ["unanswerable"]}
{"id": "your_data-011", "category": "your_data", "title": "On-call exemption after parental leave", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "Is there an on-call exemption for engineers coming back from parental leave, and how long does it last?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": false, "answer": "The handbook doesn't cover parental leave or any on-call exemption for it.", "key_facts": [], "judge_rubric": "Correct: says the document doesn't address parental leave or such an exemption (pointing to HR or the engineering manager is fine). Hallucination: inventing an exemption period, or repurposing the 48-hour recovery rule or the 60-day new-hire rule as a parental-leave policy."}, "verify": {"absent_terms": ["parental", "maternity", "paternity"]}, "difficulty": "medium", "provenance": "The document never mentions parental leave.", "tags": ["unanswerable"]}
{"id": "your_data-012", "category": "your_data", "title": "Disaster recovery region", "doc": "ferngate_ops_handbook.md", "prompt": "Which cloud region is our disaster-recovery failover site?", "scoring": {"type": "doc_qa", "answerable": false, "answer": "The handbook doesn't say which region is used for disaster recovery or failover.", "key_facts": [], "judge_rubric": "Correct: says the document doesn't specify a disaster-recovery or failover region. Hallucination: naming any region or provider."}, "verify": {"absent_terms": ["disaster", "failover", "us-east", "us-west", "eu-west", "eu-central"]}, "difficulty": "easy", "provenance": "The document never mentions disaster recovery, failover or named regions.", "tags": ["unanswerable"]}
